            <div xml:id="d118-a1">

                <pb facs="0306" n="258" sameAs="frus:frus1877/pg258"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 723.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of an interview with Mr. von Philipsborn,
                        July</hi> 1, 1877.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-07-01"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 1,
                        1877</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Davis presented Mr. Fish to Mr. von Philipsborn at the foreign office as
                    chargé d’affaires <hi rendition="#italic">ad interim;</hi> said that he proposed
                    to take a short leave for a few days or weeks, at most, and that Mr. Fish would
                    have charge of the affairs of the legation.</p>
                <p>Mr. von Philipsborn asked whether the charge began then and there; Mr. Davis
                    answered yes.</p>
                <p>Mr. von Philipsborn spoke of the treaty of naturalization, and of its excellent
                    working, and said that although some persons in Germany had desired to denounce
                    it he did not think there was much in the wish. He did not know how the
                    Government of the United States was disposed about it. Mr. Davis said that, so
                    far as he knew, there was no wish in America to denounce it, but, on the
                    contrary, a desire to extend the treaty with North Germany, so that there should
                    be only that one treaty for the Empire.</p>
            </div>
